\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2015\par 11:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNB Still Prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Official Says\par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc fell after a senior official at the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  was quoted as saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relatively weak.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rating Slips to Junk, Ruble Takes a Beating\par \par \uc2 Russia's fractured economy suffered another potential blow after credit-rating firm \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  cut the country's rating to junk, sending it below investment grade for the first time in more than a decade. The news drove the ruble even lower.\par \par \uc2 EU Heads Call for More Russia Sanctions\par \par \uc2 European Union\uc2  heads of government asked the bloc's foreign ministers to consider further sanctions on Russia as a response to the latest violence in eastern Ukraine, saying Moscow held "responsibility" for the rebels' actions.\par \par \uc2 Putin Blames Kiev for Violence\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  blamed an escalation in violence in eastern Ukraine on the pro-Western government in Kiev, taking a hard line as U.S. and European leaders said they could tighten sanctions against Russia unless it reins in pro-Russian militants there.\par \par \uc2 China Lowers 2015 Industrial-Output Growth to Around 8%\par \par \uc2 China has lowered its target for industrial-output growth to around 8% this year from 9.5% in 2014, an official at the industrial ministry said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 U.K. Economy Cools\par \par \uc2 The U.K. economy slowed more than expected in the final quarter of 2014 as construction activity slipped and tumbling oil prices hit North Sea oil and gas production.\par \par \uc2 Russia, Kazakhstan Acquire More Gold\par \par \uc2 The central banks of Russia, Kazakhstan and Belarus topped up their official gold reserves last month, while countries including Tajikistan and Mozambique cashed in some of their holdings as gold prices rose, data from the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  show.\par \par \uc2 Oil Prices in Flux After \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Comment\par \par \uc2 Oil prices fluctuated between gains and losses after comments by the head of \uc2 OPEC\uc2  failed to reverse bearish sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Obama to Propose Opening More Areas to Drilling\par \par \uc2 The Obama administration is planning to propose opening up new areas of the nation's federally owned waters to oil and natural gas drilling, including areas along the Atlantic Coast, according to people familiar with the plan.\par \par \uc2 Keystone Pipeline Bill Stalls in Senate\par \par \uc2 The Senate\uc2  on Monday rejected a procedural motion to advance legislation to approve the Keystone XL pipeline, prolonging the chamber's debate over the project.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 27, 2015 05:55 ET (10:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150127eb1r0014v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
