\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-War-torn Ukraine sees highest inflation in 14 years\b0\par\par\uc2 515 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2015\par 15:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Rises in gas prices, hryvnia devaluation, play a big part\par \par \uc2 * High inflation reflects year of turmoil and war\par \par \uc2 * High inflation seen for 2015 if gas price raised further (Adds detail, background)\par \par \uc2 KIEV, Jan 6 (Reuters) - Sharp rises in the domestic price of gas and the devaluation of the hryvnia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushed up inflation in Ukraine to almost 25 percent last year, its highest level in 14 years.\par \par \uc2 Official statistics published on Tuesday reflected the year of turmoil in the country of 45 million in which street protests ousted a Moscow-backed president, prompting Russia to annex Crimea and support a separatist rebellion in which more than 4,700 people have been killed.\par \par \uc2 The numbers also reflect the reform policies of the pro-Western government of Prime Minister Arseny Yatseniuk who is seeking to uncouple the economy from its subsidised Soviet past and make it fit for competition in the European mainstream.\par \par \uc2 The State Statistic Service said consumer price inflation soared to 24.9 percent from 0.5 percent in 2013 - close to the 25.8 percent recorded in 2000 when Ukraine had to absorb the effect of a global crisis.\par \par \uc2 With the war in the east hitting major exports such as steel and forcing for the first time imports of coal and electricity, a major factor driving up inflation was the government's decision to raise the price of gas in homes across the country, largely at the behest of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  , on which Ukraine relies for a steady stream of credit under a $17 billion bail-out programme, has long been pressing for an end to state subsidies to the state-run energy behemoth Naftogaz which it sees as running counter to the economy's long term benefit.\par \par \uc2 But the devaluation in the hryvnia, which lost half of its value in 2014, also helped push up the price of food, fuel and services.\par \par \uc2 Under the former government of Mykola Azarov the hryvnia was held at around 8 to the U.S. dollar by regular \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and supported by regular foreign financial credits notably from \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  introduced a flexible exchange rate last April, the hryvnia has since dropped to around 15.7 to the dollar with largely-unchanged salaries and pay losing purchasing ability.\par \par \uc2 The 2015 budget, now signed by President \uc2 Petro Poroshenko\uc2 , envisages inflation of just over 13 percent in 2015 though \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Valeriya Gontareva\uc2  has said it is likely to rise to as much as 18 percent if the government raises home gas prices further as requested by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  itself is to visit Ukraine later this week to assess prospects for the and review the planned reforms.\par \par \uc2 The government expects the visit to unlock three tranches of new and overdue credit, possibly totalling more than $4 billion, with the bail-out programme possibly being expanded beyond $17 billion. (Reporting by Natalia Zinets; Writing by Richard Balmforth Editing by Jeremy Gaunt) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-1-6T15:14:52.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150106eb1600d7x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
