\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Year-end caution subdues shares, lifts yen\b0\par\par\uc2 835 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2014\par 14:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * European stocks down, euro at near 2-1/2 year low\par     * Oil, copper drop; dollar index hovers near April 2006 high\par     * Yen rallies, Nikkei drops in year-end risk aversion\par     * Greek bond yields steady after angst over early poll\par     * Wall Street expect to edge back from record highs\par \par     By Marc Jones\par     LONDON, Dec 30 (Reuters) - A wave of risk aversion swept\par through global markets on Tuesday as general end-of-year caution\par and worries about Greece's future in the euro zone subdued\par shares and lifted the safe-haven yen and gold.\par     There was also a new 5-1/2 year low for oil prices\par   as persistent worries about a global supply glut\par amplified the downward pressure of its pricing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the\par dollar, as it hovered near an 8-1/2 year high. \par     Wall Street, where the S&P 500 notched its latest\par record high on Monday, was expected to start on the back foot\par after Europe's stock markets spent their day firmly in red. \par     Britain's FTSE 100, Germany's DAX and\par France's CAC 40 were down 1, 1.2 and 1.1 percent\par respectively before U.S. trading after a 1.6 percent drop for\par Tokyo's Nikkei in its final session of 2014 had wilted\par Asia.\par     But Greece's bond yields, a proxy of the\par government's borrowing costs, steadied as investors took a\par relatively sanguine view of snap elections that are likely to\par empower a party seeking to flout international bailout terms.\par   \par     The left-wing Syriza party, which opposes the European\par Union/International Monetary Fund bailout, has\par said it wants to abandon the many of the drastic spending cuts\par that are central to Greece's rehabilitation programme.\par     "The developments in Greece have prompted some concerns\par among global investors, at least in the near term, which is\par boosting safe-haven demand for the yen," said Lee Hardman a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     "It's probably fair to say Greece could leave the euro and\par it would have less of an impact than in 2012, but it would be  \par dangerous," he said.\par     But if market reaction is anything to go by, investors see\par the threat posed by Greece to the euro zone as far better\par contained than the first time around. The region has set up a\par new banking watchdog this year and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is\par scoping out government bond buying.\par     Italy, the bloc's third biggest economy with some of the\par biggest difficulties, saw its 10-year borrowing costs fall to a\par record low of under 1.9 percent at auction on Tuesday as Spanish\par market yields also hit new lows.     \par     The euro, held just above a 2-1/2 year low at $1.2160\par as more lacklustre bank lending data and fresh evidence of\par deflation taking hold in Spain and Italy further bolstered the\par case for ECB action.  \par     \par     \par     OIL SLUMP\par     Oil prices, the other big focus for world markets at\par present, extended their sharp recent falls in early European\par trading as they dropped below $57 per barrel for the first time\par since May 2009.\par     Having officially halved in price in the last six months,\par Brent crude fell 98 cents to $56.90 after hitting $56.74\par earlier in the session, while U.S. crude fell 77 cents to\par $52.84 a barrel. \par     An industry group, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 American Petroleum Institute\f28 \uc2 \f2 , is\par scheduled to release its inventory report later in the day ahead\par of \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Department of Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  data on Wednesday. \par     In a cautious \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the yen made sharp\par gains against both the dollar and euro as investors sought the\par traditional safety of the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     As U.S. trading gathered momentum it was up almost one\par percent at 119.45 yen to the dollar as the dollar itself held\par just below an 8-1/2 year high against six of the world's main\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     Europe's benchmark safe haven, the 10-year German Bund\par , was slightly softer on day but was heading for\par its biggest annual fall in yields since 2008. It was last at\par 0.546 percent, roughly a quarter of the 2.19 percent of U.S.\par Treasuries. \par     Gold also nudged higher but the dollar's broad-based\par strength meant more pain for other commodities. Copper \par edged down to $6,280.25 a tonne, after falling to its lowest\par level in four-and-a-half years this week.\par     Worries about China's economy added downward pressure.\par Growth in China's manufacturing sector likely slowed to a\par 18-month low in December, a Reuters poll showed. \par     The yo-yoing on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's markets also continued as signs of\par fresh \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lifted the rouble 3\par percent and Ukraine, some of whose bonds Russia owns, said it\par expected to get overdue \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  loans by the end of next\par month. \par \par  (Additional Reporting by Lisa Twaronite in Tokyo; Editing by\par Jon Boyle/Ruth Pitchford)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-12-30T14:55:18.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141230eacu007qf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
