\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b \uc2 Ruble Strengthens After  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Acts\b0\par\par\uc2 By ANDREY OSTROUKH, ALEXANDER KOLYANDR and CHIARA ALBANESE \par \uc2 948 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2014\par 12:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top North American Equities Stories\par \uc2 DJTNAE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--Russia's battered ruble recovered some of its recent losses against the dollar on Wednesday as the market welcomed measures by the Bank of Russia to shore up the country's banks, and the Finance Ministry said it would start selling its excess foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings.\par \par \uc2 In a volatile day of trading, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained more than 10% on the Moscow Exchange after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a raft of measures to ease banking regulations and aimed at convincing Russians to keep their money in the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ruble was trading around 60 to the dollar by late Wednesday evening. The dollar-denominated RTS Index also spiked 17.6%, its largest gain in five years.\par \par \uc2 The strengthening of the ruble was likely helped by a recovery in oil prices, with Brent crude, the global benchmark edging back above the psychologically significant $60-a-barrel level to reach $62.44 a barrel in late afternoon European trading on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Still, analysts said the ruble was far from out of the woods after a selloff in recent days that has taken its drop in value against the dollar since the start of the year to around half, amid plunging oil prices and Western sanctions over \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the finance ministry and the government face a massive challenge, and the next few weeks, if not days, could decide if a full-scale financial crisis unfolds triggered by the selloff," said Piotr Matys, rates strategist at Rabobank.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement came after Prime Minister Dmitry Medvedev cautioned Russia's top exporters to behave "responsibly" and manage their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues in a way not to increase ruble volatility.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, the Finance Ministry said it "considers the ruble to be extremely undervalued and has started selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from its balances on the market."\par \par \uc2 The ministry later said its holdings total about $7 billion, but it didn't specify how much it would sell. "We'll be selling for as long as we need to," the Interfax news agency quoted Deputy Finance Minister Alexei Moiseyev as saying.\par \par \uc2 Even Nikolai Patrushev, Russia's security chief, chipped in, presenting Russian President Vladimir Putin with his suggestions on how to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, according to Interfax.\par \par \uc2 "Things are finely balanced," said Tom Levinson chief strategist for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rates at Sberbank CIB. "President Putin's major annual speech tomorrow also means people will bide their demand."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has vastly greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $416 billion, showed no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market Wednesday, traders said. Its limited sales of dollars and euros in recent days--it spent $1.96 billion on Monday--haven't been enough to slow the ruble's drop.\par \par \uc2 A 6.5-point interest rate rise to 17% on Monday night did little to calm the markets Tuesday, and the ruble plunged to record lows of 80 a dollar, before recovering some of the lost ground late in the day.\par \par \uc2 President Putin has maintained public silence on the ruble's woes this week. His spokesman told a local newspaper Tuesday that he planned no "special statements" on the situation but would likely address it at his annual news conference, scheduled for Thursday. The spokesman, Dmitry Peskov, said Wednesday that measures were being readied in the coming days to minimize the consequences of the crisis for Russians.\par \par \uc2 Kremlin economic aide Andrei Belousov on Wednesday said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market would only calm down if the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  coordinate action to stabilize the ruble.\par \par \uc2 "I, as a participant of this process, can say that the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are seriously addressing stopping the bacchanalia on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," Mr. Belousov said, according to Interfax.\par \par \uc2 In a surprise move late Wednesday, authorities released tycoon Vladimir Yevtushenkov from house arrest, his lawyer told the Russian state news agency. Mr. Yevtushenkov, co-owner of the AFK Sistema conglomerate, was arrested and charged in September over alleged money laundering, sending shock waves through the country's business community and markets.\par \par \uc2 The government, meanwhile, is already preparing spending cuts for next year's budgets, officials said, to reflect the drop in revenue from falling oil prices and the weak economy.\par \par \uc2 Data from the Federal Statistics Service on Wednesday showed that Russian capital investment, which used to be one of the main economic growth drivers, shrank 4.8% on the year and 6.2% on the month in November, paving the way for a widely expected economic recession.\par \par \uc2 Many Russians reacted with shock this week to the huge drops in the exchange rate, gyrations reminiscent of financial crises in 2008 and 1998 that battered the economy and confidence. Banks reported surging demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with lines at branches and ATMs and some running out of dollars and euros. Interfax reported that a number of major banks in the industrial city of Chelyabinsk had run out of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cash.\par \par \uc2 Retailers also were scrambling to keep up. Major Auto, a large chain of car dealers, said it suspended sales of autos Tuesday amid the ruble moves, though many models, especially luxury brands, had already sold out. Consumers have been racing to spend their depreciating rubles on durable goods such as vehicles, appliances and electronics in recent weeks before stores raised the ruble prices.\par \par \uc2 Some fashion boutiques and jewelry shops in downtown Moscow closed their doors Wednesday, only to reopen them hours later with the prices of goods increased.\par \par \uc2 Ben Edwards in London contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTNAE1120141217each0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
