\par\par \b \uc2 New Zealand Economy Looks Solid Under Bonnet -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,216 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2014\par 23:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2248 GMT - New Zealand's economy "appears solid under the bonnet," says \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  Senior Economist Mark Smith. He notes, however, the upward surprise in the 3Q GDP data was due to factors that can be expected to at least partially unwind in 4Q. Also, the lack of pricing pressure and questions about the trade-off between economic activity and inflation "are likely to keep the official cash rate parked at 3.5% until the end of next year," says Smith. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 2222 GMT - New Zealand's economy continued to expand in 3Q, up 1% on quarter, \uc2 Statistics New Zealand\uc2  says. GDP rose 3.2% on the year. Economists had expected on-quarter growth of 0.7% and on-year growth of 3.3%. Growth in the quarter was driven by the primary industries, including agriculture and mining, which rose 5.8% on quarter. "This is some of the strongest growth in primary industries for 15 years," says national accounts manager Gary Dunnet. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 2203 GMT - \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  raises its rating on \uc2 Treasury Wine Estates\uc2  (TWE.AU) to neutral from underweight citing an improvement in sales and margins as a result of the fall in the Australian dollar. The earnings boost received from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation will increase TWE's ability to invest in promoting its brands, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  analyst Stuart Jackson says. "As a result, the execution risk on the new MD's strategy is reduced," Jackson says, lifting its price target to A$4.60/share from A$4.25. (rebecca.thurlow@wsj.com; @beckthurlow)\par \par \uc2 16:10 EST - Fed chief Yellen might have just said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to tighten in the next two meetings (January and March 2015), but some market participants are actually raising bets for a spring move. Odds for a March rate hike rise to 4%, based on Fed-fund futures, from 1% a day ago. Capital Economics is in this March camp, maintaining that labor-market improvements will force the Fed bring up its tightening timetable. Firm sees monthly payroll gains of 250k to 300k over the next few months, which should lower the jobless rate to 5.5% by March. "Under those circumstances, we still have doubts that the Fed would be prepared to delay hiking its policy rate until the middle of next year." (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 16:00 EST - Fed statement and Yellen's press conference also generated volatile trading in the interest-rate futures market. The odds of a rate increase at the Fed's June 2015 meeting earlier slipped to 19% following the statement, compared to 21% before the release. But the odds were recently at 24%. The odds were 25.87% a day earlier. A month ago the odds were 20.21%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 15:53 EST - Fed Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  doesn't seem too worried about economic turmoil in Russia, at least in terms of its potential effects on the US. Trade and financial links between the US and Russia "are actually relatively small," she says today at her press conference, and "spillovers to the United States, both through trade and financial channels, would be small." (ben.leubsdorf@wsj.com; @BenLeubsdorf)\par \par \uc2 15:44 EST - Treasury bonds posted the biggest one-day selling in three months as latest comments from Fed Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  renewed some concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may raise interest rates sooner than many investors anticipate. The selling followed a strong rally a day earlier that had sent the yield on the benchmark 10-year note to the lowest level in more than a year. Some traders said Ms. Yellen's remarks, along with stabilization in Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from recent turmoil, drove investors to cash in some chips from \uc2 the Treasury\uc2  bond market. In late-afternoon trading, the yield on the benchmark 10-year note rose to 2.146%. It rose about 0.08 percentage point, the most on a daily basis since September. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 15:36 EST - On paper, Russia has a large \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stockpile it can use as it steps up its defense against the falling ruble. In July the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said Moscow had around $470B that would cover just over a year of imports. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  since then, reducing that amount to just over $400B, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Anders Aslund, a senior fellow at the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2 , estimates that actual amount the bank can use for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely more than half that amount. Two funds \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  includes in that amount aren't technically usable and its gold and reserves held at the \uc2 IMF\uc2  aren't fungible assets that can be quickly tapped. That could limit Moscow's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the ruble to about half a year, says Lubomir Mitov, chief Europe economist at the Institute of International Finance. (ian.talley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:28 EST - The dollar popped after Yellen indicated the Fed could raise interest rates as soon as April. No hike was likely for the "next couple of meetings" and further wanted to discourage the idea the Fed would act only at meetings with press conferences. "The fact that she explicitly mentioned that policy will not be altered for two meetings, she is implying the third meeting is a go for higher rates," says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 ' \uc2 Martin Schwerdtfeger\uc2 . The dollar jumped to Y118.73 from Y117.67 earlier, putting the session's gains at 2%, while the euro has plunged to $1.2335 from $1.2405 earlier, putting it down 1.4%. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:10 EST - So what did Fed Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  mean when she said the Fed is "unlikely to begin the normalization process for at least the next couple of meetings." How many is that? She notes, after being asked by a reporter at her press conference, that the dictionary is quite clear on this subject and "a couple means two." So that would seem to make a rate hike unlikely in January or March, leaving all eyes on the Fed's next meeting after that, in April. (ben.leubsdorf@wsj.com; @BenLeubsdorf)\par \par \uc2 15:06 EST - The Canadian dollar reverses course and starts weakening during Fed Chief \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 's press conference. USD/CAD has clawed back all its losses and then some, after falling as low as C$1.1560 in the immediate minutes after the Fed issued its policy statement. Pair now at C$1.1647. Yellen says no meeting is off the table for rate increases. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 14:56 EST - Bond yields tick higher as Yellen says rate hikes are not in a predetermined path and that no meeting is off the table for rate increases. She says almost all FOMC participants expect rate increase in 2015 even though Fed signals patience in the rate statement. The 10-year note is 17/32 lower, yielding 2.132%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 17, 2014 17:48 ET (22:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141217each004c0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
