\par\par \b \uc2 As Investors Brace for Ruble's Fallout, Focus Is on Putin's Next Move\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan Cullison   \par \uc2 928 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2014\par 23:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has collapsed for the third time in less than two decades. The question now is whether its latest market meltdown will spark a global contagion, or a mayhem that Russia confines to its borders.\par \par \uc2 Russian crises have spread turmoil before. After a blowout of the ruble in 1998, the government not only defaulted on its bonds, but announced that Russian companies wouldn't be paying their debts either. Western banks were left in the lurch, and for years Russia was a pariah of credit markets.\par \par \uc2 Russia was gentler to its creditors in the world financial crisis of 2008. By then it had amassed huge foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. It blew through one-third of them to support the ruble so Russian companies could make good on debts. Russia underwent a deep recession the following year, but its creditworthiness remained mostly unscathed.\par \par \uc2 "There has already been some contagion" to the current turmoil in Russia in the form of heightened fear among emerging-market investors, said Peter Halloran, chief executive of the New York City-based Pharos Financial Group.\par \par \uc2 But how far the tumult goes is a deeper and more complex problem than simple balance sheets. Russia again has huge \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves--now the world's second largest at more than $400 billion--enough to stop the slide in the ruble and shore up markets for the near future. But whether President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , who is fighting off Western sanctions, wants to use the reserves to soothe Western creditors, is an open question.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Putin has suggested that the double whammy hitting Russian markets--collapsing oil prices along with Western sanctions in response to \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine--are a concerted Western machination to weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In response to the sanctions, he has instructed officials to prepare for an isolation from the West, a move that could influence his decision-making about repaying Western creditors and containing Russia's financial pain.\par \par \uc2 "Everyone is arguing until they're blue in the face about whether \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been handling the ruble correctly, but I don't think that is the issue," said Christopher Granville, managing director of Trusted Sources, a London-based emerging-market research firm. "Sanctions are the fundamental driver here. They are a garrote around the neck of the Russian economy."\par \par \uc2 As in Russia's first big financial blowout in 1998, the collapse of the ruble this year has been sparked by cratering oil prices. But there just about all the similarities end. In 1998, government tax collection was in shambles, and the budget was dependent on the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and bonds, most of them short-term.\par \par \uc2 Today Russia is running a budget surplus and has little debt. After the fall of the ruble this week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had enough foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to buy up all the ruble deposits in the country and still have about $100 million left over, said Charles Robertson, chief economist of Renaissance Capital in London.\par \par \uc2 Lately the fall in the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has outpaced the fall in oil, indicating that investors are worried about more than Russia's fiscal balances.\par \par \uc2 Investors in Russia say the quickened pace of the ruble decline is due to worries about Mr. Putin himself, and a political course that appears increasingly erratic. While the annexation of Crimea and support of pro-Russian rebels in eastern Ukraine has been popular, Russia's elite has been worried by the rift with the West, as well as the arrest in September of a prominent Russian oligarch on alleged money-laundering charges. Bankers note that Russia's high-net-worth depositors have been more likely to cash out of rubles and flee to dollars.\par \par \uc2 In the face of the selling, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves may not be as large as commonly perceived, said Mr. Halloran, of the Pharos Financial Group. The West's economic sanctions against Russia mean that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves must be used for a lot of things they ordinarily wouldn't be tapped for. Russian corporate borrowing has mushroomed in recent years, and Russian companies now have about $200 billon in external debt outstanding in Eurobonds.\par \par \uc2 Sanctions effectively cut off some of these companies from financial markets, "so now a lot of these things have got to be financed internally," Mr. Halloran said. "The reserves have got to be viewed now differently for Russia than they were in the past."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Halloran says he worries that Mr. Putin, facing scarce resources, could turn inwardly and plunge Russia into deeper isolation. "It wouldn't be unprecedented, when a country turns authoritarian, to refuse to pay debts in a tit-for-tat with the outside world," said Mr. Halloran, citing Argentina as an example. "The idea behind sanctions is that people will rise up against their leaders. But in fact it often has the opposite effect, and the relationship becomes unstable. People make bad decisions."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Halloran says clues to Mr. Putin's way forward should be coming soon, as he decides whether to reshuffle his government, or keep the current clique of security service colleagues that has surrounded him for years.\par \par \uc2 Another factor to watch are negotiations to establish a peace agreement in Ukraine. Any sign that Mr. Putin is softening his stance on Ukraine is a sign that he is hoping, sooner or later, to rekindle economic ties with the West.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 17, 2014 17:23 ET (22:23 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141217each00495\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
