\par\par \b \uc2 Asia central bankers on guard but feel safe from Russia  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gayatri Suroyo and Suvashree Choudhury   \par \uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2014\par 14:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA/MUMBAI, Dec 17 (Reuters) - Asian central bankers are on guard but say they are not overly concerned that the Russian rouble's collapse will trigger a big sell-off of their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying their fundamentals are favourable and their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  defences strong.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets in Asia generally firmed on Wednesday after a two-day sell-off that saw Indonesia's rupiah drop to its weakest level since the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in 1998 and Thailand's main stock index register its biggest intraday loss since October 2008.\par \par \uc2 "We're still safe," Bank Indonesia spokesman \uc2 Peter Jacobs\uc2  said on Wednesday. "Investors can see that what's causing depreciation is external factors, whereas internally we've improved."\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  said it had sold dollars to support the rupiah on Tuesday, and traders said the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  and \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  had also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic crisis should not have a big impact on Thailand," said Mathee Supapongse, a senior official at Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which left interest rates on hold on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Markets are speculating that Russia could even impose capital controls in coming days, with three retail \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading platforms halting trade in roubles, which would further hit risk appetite.\par \par \uc2 "The Russian situation is unique," said Dan Martin at Capital Economics in Singapore. "But the fear is that it just shatters global confidence and raises general risk aversion."\par \par \uc2 "In Asia, Indonesia looks the most vulnerable. It's still got its big current-account deficit," Martin added.\par \par \uc2 Despite recent falls, the rupiah might still be a little overvalued, he said.\par \par \uc2 However, \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor, \uc2 Mirza Adityaswara\uc2 , told reporters on Wednesday there was no need for panic about the rupiah's fall, describing the country's economy as "sound" and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as undervalued.\par \par \uc2 'LESSONS LEARNED'\par \par \uc2 India also has a current-account deficit, and the rupee fell to a 13-month low against the dollar on Wednesday on fears of foreign selling. It recovered later on likely \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to traders.\par \par \uc2 "The current fall in the rupee is in line with all emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and we are no different," said a source with direct knowledge of the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 's exchange rate policy.\par \par \uc2 "We are less worried as overall market conditions are better, like fiscal condition is within control, current account deficit is low, inflation is falling," the official said.\par \par \uc2 Along with the rouble rout, investors are waiting to see what guidance the U.S. Federal Reserve gives later on Wednesday on the timing of an eventual rise in U.S. interest rates, which could draw yet more funds away from Asia and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Still, Asia's central banks are much better placed to deal with exchange-rate pressures than they were during the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in the late 1990s.\par \par \uc2 "The lessons have been learned," said Khoon Goh, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2 . "A lot of central banks have built up \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as an insurance policy."\par \par \uc2 This means they should be better able to weather capital flight without resorting to capital controls. They have also put in place \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap arrangements, including the multilateral Chiang Mai Initiative, to bolster \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  defences.\par \par \uc2 "In Asia we coordinate in a way that we help each other," said Jacobs at \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 . "In case the rupiah tumbled and our \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves dropped, we could borrow using our bilateral swaps or the Chiang Mai Initiative."\par \par \uc2 India remains outside the initiative, which covers the 10 members of the \uc2 Association of South East Asian Nations\uc2  (ASEAN) plus China, Japan and South Korea. But the source with knowledge of the \uc2 Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate policy hinted that it might like to become a part of it.\par \par \uc2 "There are so many discussions going on for regional swap lines. There is already a BRICs swap line," he said, referring to a swap agreement in place between the world's biggest emerging markets of Brazil, Russia, India and China.\par \par \uc2 "(And) there is an ASEAN swap line which India may join." (Additional reporting by \uc2 Rajesh Kumar Singh\uc2  in New Delhi and Orathai Siring in Bangkok; Writing by Nicholas Owen; Editing by Mark Bendeich) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-12-17T14:15:10.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141217each00f27\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
