\par\par \b \uc2 China PMI Indicates Small Firms Still Can't Get Loans -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2014\par 03:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Further weakness in the \uc2 HSBC\uc2 /Markit flash Purchasing Managers' Index, which fell to 49.5 in December from 50.0 in November, suggests that smaller Chinese companies continue to face funding problems despite a significant increase in new bank loans in November, said CIMB economist Fan Zhang. This transmission problem dovetails with conversations his firm has had with bank managers. A reading above 50 indicates expansion with a figure below that signals contraction. "The funding conditions with smaller companies are not as expected after the interest rate cut," Zhang said. "This clearly suggests they have to do more easing and more fiscal support." (mark.magnier@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @MarkMagnier) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY is "on the verge of entering a full-fledged adjustment phase," due to the risk-aversion mood caused by the oil price tumble, says Osamu Takashima, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Citigroup Global Markets Japan\uc2  in a morning note. "The possibility of (the USD/JPY) uptick we have been assuming over the short-term will disappear" if the pair breaks below points on two technical charts simultaneously, Takashima says. The USD/JPY (now at 117.90) is already below the 118.12 base line on an Ichimoku chart, but its relative strength index (now at 53%) is still above the 50% line on a chart tracking market momentum. The pair could drop quickly if it falls below both of these lines. "Depending on the circumstances, the downside support could be around 114," Takashima says. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0146 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  saw no grounds to change the course of monetary policy, minutes of its last meeting showed, even as markets priced in a greater chance of further rate cuts next year. Minutes from the Dec. 2 meeting says "members considered that the most prudent course was likely to be a period of stability in inflation rates." It added that the current stance of monetary policy "continued to be appropriate for fostering sustainable growth in demand and inflation outcomes consistent with the target." The RBA notes that while subdued labor-market conditions were likely to persist, the inflation outlook remained within the 2%-3% target range and a buoyant housing market would likely support household consumption. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0139 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CNH Intraday: bullish bias above 6.185. The pair remains unchanged this morning, and a support base at 6.185 has formed and has allowed for a temporary stabilisation. As long as 6.185 is not broken, the pair is more likely to challenge the nearest key resistance at 6.205 at first, if breakout, look for further advance to 6.217. To sum up, suggest LONG positions above 6.185 with targets @ 6.205 & 6.217 in extension. Below 6.185 look for further downside with 6.17 & 6.148 as targets. China's holdings of US Treasuries fell by $13.6 billion month-over-month to a 20-month low of $1.25 trillion in October, according to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 US Treasury Department\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Trading Central is a commentary service specialising in technical analysis. For more information about TRADING Central please call by phone: London office (+44) 207 429 3980, Paris office (+33) 1 5528 8040 or email: sales@tradingcentral.com. (\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingcentral.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingcentral.com}}}\uc2 \f2 ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0135 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Nikkei is down 1.8% at 16802.24 in early trading, hit by the dollar's continued weakness (USD/JPY now 117.84), Wall Street's overnight selloff, and fears over Russia's economy, as the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively hiked the interest rate to defend its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against capital flight. "Oil prices continue to slide, and that is now the chief worry to Russia, which is essentially an oil exporting economy," says Yoshihiro Okumura, general manager at Chibagin Asset Management. "The creeping fear is that Russia may default, reminding investors of the prior Greek fiscal panic, and require a bailout. Beyond that, a 'domino effect' of worsening fiscal conditions at other oil-exporting nations may take hold. Oil prices look far from settled at the mid-$50 level, so more pain may yet be in store." \f28 \uc2 \f2 Japan Tobacco\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2914.TO), which sports a significant exposure to Russia's market, is down 1.7% a Y3,391. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0131 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Tocom RSS3 rubber futures are lower in slow trade as the yen is stronger versus the dollar Monday. A Tokyo-based analyst says Tocom rubber may face a bumpy ride this week as prices are influenced by external factors including macroeconomic cues from China, the largest importer and consumer of natural rubber. Benchmark May rubber trades Y1.0 lower at Y202.9/kg. (huileng.tan@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @huileng_tan) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0129 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Taiwan shares are up 0.2% at 9003.72, as investors are turning optimistic about the outlook of a planned capital gains tax on major stock investors, says a local trader. "Many investors pin hopes that the legislature will likely remove or revise the planned tax on Wednesday so major market players will return to the market," the trader says. But he says low trading volume will likely cap the market's rising momentum. Tech heavyweights are mixed; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (2330.TW) is down 0.7% at NT$135.00, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hon Hai Precision\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2317.TW) falls 0.3% to NT$87.40 but HTC (2498.TW) is up 1.4% at NT$147.50. Cathay Financial (2882.TW) gains 0.4% at NT$45.95 but Fubon Financial (2881.TW) is down 0.5% at NT$48.95. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0125 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Crude-oil futures are headed south again in early Asian trade Tuesday, pushing both Nymex West Texas Intermediate and Brent crude to fresh lows. Oil prices have now lost 47-48% since June. On tap for markets today is the China manufacturing data that could affect market sentiment. "Crude oil markets were down, as fear continued to drive bearish sentiment," ANZ says. It says the oil market is still focused on comments from the UAE energy minister that \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  would refrain from curbing output if prices fell as low as $40/bbl. Nymex WTI is down 45 cents at $55.46/bbl, Brent crude is down 55 cents at $60.51/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0125 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The New Zealand outlook is "rock solid," says ANZ senior economist \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mark Smith\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Despite a low inflation and commodity-price induced hit to the projected tax take "an improving trend is evident to the fiscal outlook, which will keep the ratings agencies on message," he adds. He says ANZ is not overly concerned "with the modest deterioration in the fiscal accounts" as public finances are moving in the right direction. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0122 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A jump in high rollers leaving Macau for other gambling destinations thanks to China's anticorruption measures and a lack of table capacity in Macau may be drawing to a close, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. Las Vegas saw sharply lower Baccarat revenue in September and October after strong growth the previous two months, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  now expects declining numbers of VIP gamblers for Australia as well. It estimates that close to 20% of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Echo Entertainment\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (EGP.AU) valuation relates to VIP gambling, a business it pegs at about A$800 million. Still, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  says that exposure is already in EGP's share price and it hikes its recommendation to neutral from underperform with a target of A$40/share. EGP is up 1.1% at A$3.69. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @RobbMStewart) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0120 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] There is only a small chance of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis spreading from Russia because the exposure of global financial markets to Moscow has already been substantially reduced, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nomura Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yunosuke Ikeda\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a morning note. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sharply increased its key interest rate Monday to 17.0% from 10.5% to stem the recent plunge in the ruble. But investors still "need to be wary of a potential heightening in worries about a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis on a regional basis" if capital outflows keep pace with the falloff in oil prices. The amount of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's foreign reserves available for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  now stands at $204.7 billion, already about 25% lower than the level in February before Moscow annexed Crimea from Ukraine. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0114 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Price expectations of Japanese companies fell slightly in the three months to December amid the recent economic downturn, a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  survey shows Tuesday, an outcome that bodes ill for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's goal of achieving 2% inflation by later next year. About 10,000 companies polled by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  say prices of consumer products will rise at an annual rate of 1.4% in one year, slightly down from 1.5% in the previous September survey. Companies also expect their sales prices to rise 1.0% in one year and 1.7% in three-years and 2.0% in five years. All three figures marked a 0.1 percentage point drop from the previous survey. A BOJ official says these falls might reflect sharp falls in oil prices in recent months.(Tatsuo.ito@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 15, 2014 21:08 ET (02:08 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141216eacg000bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscal