\par\par \b \uc2 Oil Declines Further Ahead China Factory Data -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,519 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2014\par 02:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0125 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Crude-oil futures are headed south again in early Asian trade Tuesday, pushing both Nymex West Texas Intermediate and Brent crude to fresh lows. Oil prices have now lost 47-48% since June. On tap for markets today is the China manufacturing data that could affect market sentiment. "Crude oil markets were down, as fear continued to drive bearish sentiment," ANZ says. It says the oil market is still focused on comments from the UAE energy minister that \uc2 OPEC\uc2  would refrain from curbing output if prices fell as low as $40/bbl. Nymex WTI is down 45 cents at $55.46/bbl, Brent crude is down 55 cents at $60.51/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0125 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The New Zealand outlook is "rock solid," says ANZ senior economist \uc2 Mark Smith\uc2 . Despite a low inflation and commodity-price induced hit to the projected tax take "an improving trend is evident to the fiscal outlook, which will keep the ratings agencies on message," he adds. He says ANZ is not overly concerned "with the modest deterioration in the fiscal accounts" as public finances are moving in the right direction. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0122 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A jump in high rollers leaving Macau for other gambling destinations thanks to China's anticorruption measures and a lack of table capacity in Macau may be drawing to a close, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. Las Vegas saw sharply lower Baccarat revenue in September and October after strong growth the previous two months, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  now expects declining numbers of VIP gamblers for Australia as well. It estimates that close to 20% of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Echo Entertainment\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (EGP.AU) valuation relates to VIP gambling, a business it pegs at about A$800 million. Still, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  says that exposure is already in EGP's share price and it hikes its recommendation to neutral from underperform with a target of A$40/share. EGP is up 1.1% at A$3.69. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0120 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] There is only a small chance of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis spreading from Russia because the exposure of global financial markets to Moscow has already been substantially reduced, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nomura Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yunosuke Ikeda\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a morning note. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sharply increased its key interest rate Monday to 17.0% from 10.5% to stem the recent plunge in the ruble. But investors still "need to be wary of a potential heightening in worries about a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crisis on a regional basis" if capital outflows keep pace with the falloff in oil prices. The amount of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's foreign reserves available for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  now stands at $204.7 billion, already about 25% lower than the level in February before Moscow annexed Crimea from Ukraine. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0114 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Price expectations of Japanese companies fell slightly in the three months to December amid the recent economic downturn, a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  survey shows Tuesday, an outcome that bodes ill for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's goal of achieving 2% inflation by later next year. About 10,000 companies polled by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  say prices of consumer products will rise at an annual rate of 1.4% in one year, slightly down from 1.5% in the previous September survey. Companies also expect their sales prices to rise 1.0% in one year and 1.7% in three-years and 2.0% in five years. All three figures marked a 0.1 percentage point drop from the previous survey. A BOJ official says these falls might reflect sharp falls in oil prices in recent months.(Tatsuo.ito@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0110 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The number of dairy cattle in New Zealand continued to rise, hitting 6.7 million at the end of June versus 6.5 million a year earlier, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Statistics New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Generally good pastoral conditions since the previous June contributed to the increase," agriculture manager Neil Kelly says. In the same one-year period, sheep, beef and deer numbers fell. The number of sheep declined by 1.2 million, to 29.6 million as at June 2014. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0109 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Australia's government deserves credit for not leaning further on the economy as its budget position weakens thanks largely to external factors, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. Business and consumer confidence is already subdued and fiscal challenges for the country lie ahead. Still, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  suggests more work needs to be done to achieve a sustainable budget path, with some responsibility resting on the politicians. "History will judge this government's budget performance on the outcomes, the final budget outcomes, rather than the best of plans." (robb.stewart@wsj.com; Twitter: @RobbMStewart) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0106 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CHF is likely to trade with a buoyant tone Tuesday, underpinned by improved USD sentiment after stronger-than-expected U.S. November industrial production data, and franc sales on a soft CHF/JPY cross. But USD/CHF gains are tempered by franc demand on soft GBP/CHF, AUD/CHF, NZD/CHF and CAD/CHF crosses. Daily chart is still negative-biased as MACD and stochastics bearish; five-day moving average below 15-day moving average and declining. Resistance at 0.9682 (Monday's high); breach would target 0.9699 (Friday's high), then 0.9710 (Thursday's high) and 0.9723 (Wednesday's high). Support at 0.9610 (Monday's low, near the 55-day moving average); breach would target 0.9593 (Nov. 26 low), then 0.9564 (Nov. 21 low). Spot USD/CHF is at 0.9649. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0102 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] AUD/USD gets a slight lift to 0.8224 after the RBA minutes say the inflation outlook remained consistent with the 2% to 3% target, says OM Financial's Stuart Ive. He says the pair has support at 0.8180 and resistance at 0.8270 and "still looks susceptible to a downside move." The next key event will be the \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  flash China December manufacturing PMI data (forecast 50.0) due at 0145 GMT. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0100 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] GBP/USD is likely to trade with a bearish bias Tuesday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair is undermined by improved USD sentiment, sterling sales on a soft GBP/JPY cross amid waning investor risk appetite, sterling sales on a buoyant EUR/GBP cross and on a soft GBP/CHF cross. Spotlight Tuesday is on U.K. November CPI data (forecast -0.1% on month, +1.2% on year) due at 0930 GMT. Daily chart is mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics in bearish mode. Support at 1.5599 (Monday's low). A breach would target 1.5539 (Dec. 8 low), then 1.5504 (Sept. 2, 2013 low) and 1.5426 (Aug. 28, 2013 reaction low). Resistance at 1.5690 (hourly chart), then at 1.5746 (Monday's high). A breach would tilt near-term view positive, targeting 1.5756-1.5763 band (Thursday's high-Dec. 1 high), then 1.5825 (Nov. 27 reaction high). Spot GBP/USD is at 1.5639. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0059 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/GBP is likely to consolidate with a bullish bias Tuesday after hitting a two-week high 0.7972 on Monday, as markets await U.K. November CPI data (forecast -0.1% on-month, +1.2% on-year) due at 0930 GMT. Daily chart is positive-biased as MACD and stochastics bullish; five-day moving average above 15-day moving average and advancing. Resistance at 0.7972-0.7976 (Monday's high-Dec. 1 high); breach would target 0.8002 (Nov. 21 high), then 0.8027 (Nov. 20 high, near the 200-day moving average). Support at 0.7903 (Monday's low); breach would temper positive near-term view, targeting 0.7864-0.7856 band (Friday's low-Dec. 9 low). Spot EUR/GBP is at 0.7957. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0057 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Finance Minister Bill English says Treasury's forecasts are merely forecasts and "we believe the strength of the economy and constrained government spending can deliver a surplus this year." Treasury has forecast a deficit of NZ$572 million in the year to June 2015 due to lower tax revenues on the back of a tumble in dairy prices and low inflation. English did not deny dairy prices have fallen sharply but says other facets of the economy will help offset. He did not say how big he thought a possible surplus could be but says "the overall trend in the government's accounts is positive and heading in the right direction." (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 15, 2014 20:25 ET (01:25 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141216eacg0002j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
