\par\par \b \uc2 CIBC Cuts Oil-Price Forecasts -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2014\par 00:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] CIBC lowers its oil-price forecasts but warns "the lower prices go now, the higher they will rebound later." The bank expects Brent to average $80.50/barrel in 2015, down from its September call of $108/barrel. For WTI, CIBC expects $73/barrel next year, down from its prior forecast of $98/barrel. CIBC expects the oil market to be oversupplied by 1.3 million b/d in 1H 2015. "The earliest we would expect to see price-induced changes in either supply or demand"--meaning, an \uc2 OPEC\uc2  decision or geopolitical supply shock doesn't count--"is after six months of low prices," the bank says. (nicole.friedman@wsj.com, @NicoleFriedman)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--\uc2 Genworth Financial\uc2  (GNW) disappointed investors last month when it surprised them with a higher-than-expected charge to boost claims reserves for long-term-care insurance. It has another review under way on other long-term-care reserves, and GNW is mum on the date for revealing what's what. "My prognostication is that there is little that they can tell us that will result in celebration," David Havens of Imperial Capital tells clients. He sees "a meaty charge that will be met as a market neutral to negative event." \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says the stock is "heavily debated" with a key topic "the potential magnitude" of a charge. (leslie.scism@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2350 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Nymex crude is likely to consolidate with a bearish bias near-term after hitting a five-and-a-half year low $55.02/bbl Monday, \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is negative-biased as the five- and 15-day moving averages are declining; the MACD and slow stochastic indicators are bearish, although the latter is at oversold levels. A drop below $55.02 would target the lower 20-day two-standard deviation Bollinger Band (now at $53.75), and then the psychological $50.00 line. But a rise above $58.73 (Monday's high) would temper the negative near-term view, exposing upside to $59.57 (Friday's high), then to the 10-day exponential moving average (now at $61.16), $61.68 (Thursday's high), $63.43 (Wednesday's high), $64.20 (Dec. 9 high) and $65.55 (Dec. 8 high). January crude is down 10 cents at $55.81/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2348 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Consensus EPS estimates for Australian companies in fiscal 2015 remain too high, suggests \uc2 Bell Potter Securities\uc2  Executive Director Charlie Aitken. With the \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 nearing the lower end of Aitken's 5100-5450 forecast trading range, he lowers the floor on the range to 5000. Aitken says he remains short AUD/USD and is targeting a fall to US$0.75 in quick time as Australia heads toward a new year and the likelihood of further rate cuts. He anticipates the Reserve Bank will cut its cash rate by 25 basis points in February and again by the same amount in April. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Rate rise alone may not be enough to strengthen the ruble, says Renaissance Capital. The ruble will strengthen Tuesday when the market opens after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp rate increase, "especially if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The rate rise not as important for the market as a clear sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to act. "We assume the total volume of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be at $20 billion before Dec. 25 to strengthen the ruble by 10% to 15%", says Oleg Kouzmin, economist in Russia and CIS at Renaissance Capital.(alexander.kolyandr@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Global Financial Integrity, a Washington-based research group, estimates $991 billion flowed out of developing and emerging economies in 2012. Fraudulent misinvoicing of trade transactions accounted for almost 78% of the money. Global Financial says outflows are growing at a rate of 9.4% a year, about twice as fast as global GDP. In 2012, China led the world in illegal capital outflows, followed by Russia, India and Mexico. Illicit capital flows have more than tripled from $297 billion in 2003, and at $991 billion, the sum is "greater than the combined sum of direct foreign investment and net official development assistance." (jose.decordoba@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] While \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade is closed in Russia for the night, futures on the ruble with delivery in March gain 8% after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate rise to RUB63.93 from RUB68.97 per dollar on the Chicago Mercantile Exchange. (alexander.kolyandr@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  reckons \uc2 Newcrest Mining\uc2  (NCM.AU) can only afford to pursue the joint development of the Golpu gold project in Papua New Guinea if dividend payouts remain suspended, the maturity of debt is extended beyond fiscal 2020 and its ownership is cut to 35% from the current 50%. NCM and Harmony Gold (HAR.JO) are moving forward with plans for a staged development of Golpu. Deutsche retains a sell recommendation on NCM but nudges its price target up 2.5% to A$8.30/share as it includes Golpu in its modelling. NCM is trading down 2.8% at A$10.37. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 2330 GMT - STW Communications' (SGN.AU) warning it will likely miss full-year earnings guidance due to weaker client commitments prompts \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  to trim its profit forecast by 8% to A$48M and its 2015 forecast by 9% to A$49.5M. SGN expects next year to be challenging given broader macro conditions, subdued advertising expenditure and weak consumer confidence. Deutsche expects a clearer picture on the outlook for 2015 from the company in February. It has a buy recommendation and A$1.20/share target. SGN down 0.5% at A$0.945. (robb.stewart@wsj.com; @RobbMStewart)\par \par \uc2 18:17 EST - \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  (KO) says today it expects to buy back $2B to $3B of its shares next year. That's in line with its guidance for $2.5B in buybacks in 2014, but also gives it wiggle room to adjust on the fly. One of those variables is potential M&A activity, CFO \uc2 Kathy Waller\uc2  tells analysts in a financial modeling conference call, adding KO continues to look at potential acquisitions "opportunistically." KO agreed to buy minority stakes this year in \uc2 Keurig Green Mountain\uc2  (GMCR) and \uc2 Monster Beverage\uc2  (MNST), part of a diversification push amid slowing soda sales growth. (mike.esterl@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 18:12 EST - \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  (KO) said today it expects 4%-5% EPS growth in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -neutral terms this year and roughly the same next year. That's in line with investor expectations after KO warned in October it would fall short of its HSD target in 2014, with no improvement in 2015. CFO \uc2 Kathy Waller\uc2  reiterated in a financial modeling call with analysts after the closing bell the company doesn't expect sales volumes to improve 4Q vs 3Q, hurt in part by continued macroeconomic challenges in key emerging markets like China and Brazil. "The same trends are continuing," said Waller. KO off a fraction at $40.40 after hours. (mike.esterl@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:55 EST - Shipments of home appliances in the US shows slower growth in the latest month. The \uc2 Association of Home Appliance Manufacturers\uc2  reports that wholesale shipments of the so-called Big Six appliance categories -- washers, dryers, dishwashers, refrigerators, freezers and ranges and ovens -- in the four weeks ended Nov. 22 were up 3% from a year earlier to 3.6M units. That's down from 6.5% growth in the four weeks to Oct. 25. For the year's first 11 months, shipments are up 5%. (bob.hagerty@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 15, 2014 18:56 ET (23:56 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141215eacf004bp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
