\par\par \b \uc2 Macro Horizons: Oil Price Rout Continues, Signaling Weak Global Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2014\par 13:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey and Alen Mattich\par \par \uc2 Macro Horizons covers the main macroeconomic and policy news events affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed income and equity markets around the world, as selected by editors in New York, London and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 WRAP: Oil prices are once again front and center for markets today, with prices dropping below $60 a barrel after the \uc2 International Energy Agency\uc2  produced a pessimistic assessment of the outlook for world crude demand. The worrying thing about this report from the IEA -- and others earlier this week from the U.S. EIA and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  -- is that it underscores the demand component of the oil price rout. It isn't just extra supply from U.S. producers that's driving prices lower, it's that the global economy is weak. That's a less benign way to view the price drop and it's one reason markets aren't entirely happy with what it is signaling. (MC)\par \par \uc2 CHINA: November industrial output was up 7.2% on-year, down from +7.7% on-year in October and against 7.5% expected. Retail sales were up 11.7%, compared with a gain of 11.5% in October.\par \par \uc2 The improvement in retail sales could be a hint that China is making progress in the challenging task of building a consumer-led economy, but the unexpected drop in industrial production is a reminder of the cost of engineering that otherwise vital transition. With Chinese industry still very much in a state of overcapacity, the necessary reversal in investment has produced a slowdown in output. That is in part what is hurting global demand for commodities and why oil prices are plunging. (MC)\par \par \uc2 OIL: \uc2 International Energy Agency\uc2  cut its 2015 forecast for global oil demand by 230,000 barrels a day to 900,000 barrels a day.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices fell firmly below $60 a barrel after the IEA slashed its demand forecast. The collapse in prices has put producers under significant pressure, roiling their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors panic about their economic prospects. And there looks to be no end in sight until the global economy starts to generate strong growth--still a distant prospect. (AM)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 RUSSIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 : The \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets again on Friday to defend the ruble against further falls.\par \par \uc2 Plummeting oil prices and a stuttering economy amid Western sanctions continue to bedevil the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But however much the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruble's stability seems to be short-lived. (AM)\par \par \uc2 FRANCE: October current account deficit narrowed to EUR900 million from EUR1.2 billion in September.\par \par \uc2 A rise in France's services surplus helped trim the country's overall trade deficit, though the goods deficit remained unchanged at EUR2.8 billion. France's best hope for short-term growth remains an expansion of its trade position, which, given the weakness of the rest of the euro zone and the global economy generally, seems unlikely in the near term. (AM)\par \par \uc2 COMING UP:\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 8:30 a.m. EST. November producer price index. [Expected PPI -0.1% on-month vs. +0.2% in October; core PPI expected +0.1%0 on-month vs. +0.4%.]\par \par \uc2 The big factor here is the fall in oil and gasoline prices, which inevitably will drag down the headline PPI number. There may also be evidence that lower energy prices are now bleeding into the overall index and imposing broader disinflationary effects. That trend will continue to give the Federal Reserve wiggle room and allow it to take its time in pushing rates higher. (MC)\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 10 a.m. EST. Thomson Reuters / \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  December mid-month November consumer sentiment index. [Expected 90.0 vs. 88.8 end-October.]\par \par \uc2 The stars are aligned for continued improvement in consumer sentiment: gasoline prices are much lower, stocks are hitting records and the job market is improving. (MC)\par \par \uc2 To sign up for this email, click here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/MacroHorizonsSignup"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/MacroHorizonsSignup}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 12, 2014 07:24 ET (12:24 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141212eacc001it\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
