\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b \uc2 Russian  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Raises Rates to Address Sliding Ruble\b0\par\par\uc2 By ANDREY OSTROUKH and JAMES MARSON \par \uc2 819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2014\par 11:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Global Market Stories\par \uc2 DJTGMS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--The Bank of Russia raised interest rates for the fifth time this year, but the move did little to ease pressure on the battered ruble as the regulator warned that Russia faces two years of stagnation and high inflation.\par \par \uc2 The bank's statement after its board met on Thursday was the latest stark acknowledgment by Russia's leadership that its economy will face protracted pain amid falling oil prices and a confrontation with the West over Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The hike in the bank's key rate of 100 basis points to 10.5%, smaller than many analysts forecast, wasn't enough to stem the slide of the ruble, which fell to an all-time low of 55.35 against the dollar after the announcement.\par \par \uc2 The regulator's cautious move highlights the dilemma it faces: A larger hike could further damage the already limping economy and hurt living standards.\par \par \uc2 The bank is "loathe to do anything radical--and is like a rabbit caught in the headlights," said Timothy Ash, an analyst at Standard Bank in London.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Russia Chairwoman Elvira Nabiullina said the ruble's drop after was a reasonable market reaction, and that she expected the ruble to recover significantly next year. She said that the bank was careful with monetary tightening and avoided a bigger rate rise given that the economy is on the brink of recession.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "found itself between a rock and a hard place with today's decision," said Tom Levinson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Sberbank CIB. "It is a compromise outcome that does not give much clarity to a market desperate for some."\par \par \uc2 The ruble has lost more than 40% of its value against the U.S. dollar this year. Falling prices for oil, Russia's chief export, and the impact of Western sanctions over \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine have pushed the economy toward recession, prompting investors to sell ruble-denominated assets such as stocks and bonds. Russian companies' dollar purchases ahead of end-of-year debt repayments have also weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday increased its key rate to 10.5% from 9.5% and the deposit rate to 9.5% from 8.5%. The fixed repo rate, which serves as a ceiling for money-market rates, was raised to 11.5% from 10.5%. Analysts said a further hike of 100 basis points was likely in January.\par \par \uc2 After hitting record lows, the ruble briefly strengthened in what traders said looked like an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it quickly weakened again to more than 55 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Nabiullina said the ruble is undervalued, even at current oil prices, by up to 20%, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should strengthen next year even if oil prices are around $60 per barrel. She forecast oil at $80 per barrel in 2015.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it expects annual inflation to reach 10% by the end of the year and push even higher in the first quarter of 2015. The bank had targeted 6.5% inflation this year.\par \par \uc2 The economy will grow 0.6% this year, then stagnate for two years, but could grow in 2017, the bank said. Despite numerous calls from businessmen and industrialists for cheaper lending, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly tightened monetary policy and has pledged to rein in rising prices. The bank has now hiked its key rate by 500 basis points this year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't announce any further measures to support the ruble. It stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the ruble's fall in November after selling almost $80 billion to little effect, although it has sold some $5 billion in ad hoc \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month. Daria Isakova from Otkritie expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit ruble liquidity in the next two weeks to stem demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the bank has few further options to support the ruble. Ms. Nabiullina reiterated that the bank didn't intend to introduce administrative restrictions, which can include capital controls.\par \par \uc2 Piotr Matys, a rates strategist at Rabobank, said the rates hike "isn't enough to stabilize the ruble and increases the risk of a full-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 He said Russia may be heading for a financial crisis more serious than the recession the bank appeared to be trying to stave off by slowly raising rates.\par \par \uc2 Neil Shearing, chief emerging markets economist at Capital Economics, said Russia's banks look vulnerable to a weaker ruble.\par \par \uc2 The regulator "has to tread a fine line between tolerating a weaker ruble and not allowing it to spiral out of control," he said in a report.\par \par \uc2 Alexander Kolyandr in Moscow and Chiara Albanese in London contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com and James Marson at james.marson@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTGMS1120141211eacb0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict