\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's BNDES May Help Economy by Shrinking -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2014\par 14:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:10 EST - \uc2 Brazil's development bank\uc2  has been a major part of economic policy in recent past as a channel through which the government lent billions of reais at subsidized rates to spur growth. But the economy languished anyway, and now the BNDES could still be very important, only for the opposite reason. Newly appointed Finance Minister \uc2 Joaquim Levy\uc2 , who is still to be ensconced, has pledged to reduce Treasury transfers to state banks. He needs to do so to balance government accounts. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a bet Levy meant what he said, noted today it expects subsidized credit to diminish, helping the fight against inflation. It all points to lower BNDES activity in the future, analysts say. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1309 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Indian financial services sector dominated fund raising through private placement of debt during the April-September period, says Prime Database. Indian companies' raised 1.59 trillion rupees ($26 billion) through private placement in the first half of the current fiscal year, up 22% from a year earlier. Nearly three-quarters of this amount was raised by the financial services sector. Power sector ranked second with a 9% share in total fund raising. Fund raising by the private sector too jumped 36% to 700.31 billion rupees, the data shows. Public sector units, however, saw a drop in fund raising by 30% to 89.12 billion rupees, it says. The top three fund raising were by \uc2 Housing Development Finance Corp\uc2 , \uc2 Rural Electrification Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Power Finance Corp\uc2 . (nupur.acharya@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1304 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Today's SNB decision suggests yet again that despite a substantial deterioration in the economic and inflation outlook, Switzerland's preferred stance remains defend the 1.20 floor with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "We now expect EUR/CHF to remain stuck close to the perceived SNB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels of just above 1.2010 or so for the coming weeks at least," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Beat Siegenthaler, adding that the SNB has warned that further measures could be taken 'immediately' but it would seem clear that outside formal decision dates this will not easily happen. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1258 GMT Just a few hours after a 1% rate hike in its main policy rate, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to stem the ruble's freefall, which hit a fresh record low just after the rate decision. But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been tiny, says Tim Ash at Standard Bank. "We estimate not much more than $300 million. That's small change--like an oligarch's ski trip expenses for the weekend. It has not really had much impact frankly, and the CBR rate hike, disappointed, as expected" he says. The ruble is currently at 55.20 per U.S. dollar at 1255 GMT. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:53 EST - Bank of Canada Gov. Poloz reiterates in a speech in New York that it will take another couple of years before the Canadian economy can enjoy "steady, natural growth" with inflation sustainably at the 2% target. He also repeats it appears the export growth and investment spending the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has long been hoping for may have finally begun. Nonetheless, Poloz says the global economy is at a "critical juncture," and policies will need to remain stimulative until headwinds subside and the rebuilding phase gets underway--which would require a substantial pickup in the creation of new firms, in the process fostering job creation. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:46 EST - Anyone hoping for the US economic recovery to have positive ripple effects elsewhere continues to be disappointed. A big drop in Japan machinery orders suggests the recession brought on by April's sales-tax hike might be harder to shake than thought. In the eurozone, deflationary signals and a tepid response by banks to a new ECB lending program underscore the economic stagnation putting pressure on the ECB to do more. Yet the legacy of inflation built up from the previous cycle and the existence of strict targets are holding back some central banks--like Korea and New Zealand--which might otherwise be more aggressive right now. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is the worst situation--an economy in freefall and a plunging ruble, the latter forcing rates hikes even though they impose even more economic pain. (michael.casey@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1232 GMT Over the past ten years, lending by India's banks to the infrastructure sector has grown at a cumulated average growth rate of 36%, higher than overall credit growth of 22%, says Manas Agarwal, co-chairman of the roads, ports and other infrastructure committee of lobby group PHD Chamber of Commerce and Industry. In a report co-authored with rating agency CRISIL, he says banks's share of credit to infrastructure has risen from 4.8% in 2004 to 14% in 2014. This "poses the risk of an asset-liability mismatch given that the infrastructure project loans have long tenures," Agarwal says. Infrastructure projects need 10-15 years to return capital while bank deposits typically have a maturity of less than three years, hence the mismatch, he explains. (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:30 EST - Following yesterday's beatdown, US stock-index futures are slightly higher. The declines left the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  a whole 2.4% below Friday's record highs, which no doubt will the again gin up the "the top is in" chatter. In fact, Global Equities Research said yesterday that's the case and that stocks will ultimately retrace to 2009's lows. That would mean a 2/3 tumble from current levels. As for the here and now, there's a lot of US economic data this morning--allowing for various constituencies to pull asset classes in myriad directions. S&P 500 futures are up 3 as oil rebounds some 1% after yesterday's drubbing and gold drops 0.5%. The euro eases to $1.2463 and Treasurys gain further, putting the 10-year yield at 2.14%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:30 EST - A survey of academics, consultants and executives by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  finds expectations that policy makers will stand pat on rates today at 3% but enact a quarter-point cut within 5 months. The survey also notes views are for a 0.2% drop in CPI this month while GDP is seen rising 1.8% this year and 2.7% in 2015. (robert.kozak@wsj.com; @robertkozak2)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1229 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  have changed their ECB forecasts following a disappointing takeup of the ECB's program to provide cheap loans to banks (the TLTRO). They now expect a EUR500 billion government bond purchase program to be announced in January. "With today's second TLTRO seeing take-up of a relatively low EUR130 billion, the gap between the balance sheet intention and what the existing measures can achieve looks very big," \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts said in a research note. "As a result, we now think that the ECB is likely to push through a decision for sovereign QE." They also expect corporate bond purchases and sweeteners to the TLTROs to make them more attractive to banks. brian.blackstone@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1227 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The decision of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Russia\f28 \uc2 \f2  to raise its key rate by 100 bps to 10.5% matched \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 's forecast, the bank says, with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pointing to an acceleration in inflation as inflation expectations have surged due to ruble depreciation and external trade restrictions. That said, further rate hikes will be needed for the ruble to stabilize and to allow inflation to decelerate, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  adds. It expects a further 100 basis points of hikes in the first quarter of 2015 bringing the key policy rate to 11.5%. (alexander.kolyandr@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1217 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised its economic growth outlook for Poland for both 2014 and 2015, expecting it to expand by 3.3% this year and 3.1% in the following year due higher-then-expected growth in 3Q 2014, slightly better outlook for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  economy and significant drops in oil prices. The bank expects pace of expansion will bottom out in 1Q 2015, when it will hit 2.6%. Earlier \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  saw Poland's expasion at 2.8% in 2014 and 2.9% in 2015. (patryk.wasilewski@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 11, 2014 08:10 ET (13:10 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141211eacb0020c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
