\par\par \b \uc2 Macro Horizons: Key Your Eye on the Wage Data\b0\par\par\uc2 745 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2014\par 13:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey and Alen Mattich\par \par \uc2 Macro Horizons covers the main macroeconomic and policy news events affecting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed income and equity markets around the world, as selected by editors in New York, London and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 WRAP: As always, the market will pay rapt attention to the U.S. employment report due out at 8:30 a.m. EST Friday, but in many respects the month-to-month progress of payrolls and unemployment is just noise, with seasonal effects likely to distract from a clear picture. Yet there's ample evidence by now that the U.S. labor market is improving. What's lacking is clear proof that wages are being pushed higher by this. Some point to early indicators of wage pressures; others say there's still enough slack in the labor market to keep wages depressed. Either way, the hourly earnings number in Friday's report is becoming an increasingly import sub-indicator to watch. If it shows signs of a pickup, then the pressure will build on the Federal Reserve to hasten its move toward a rate increase early next year. (MC)\par \par \uc2 EUROZONE: Third-quarter GDP growth was 0.2% on the previous quarter, unchanged on initial estimates.\par \par \uc2 The eurozone barely managed to grow in the third quarter, having expanded just 0.1% in the previous quarter. Weak investment spending remains a drag on the economy. The data give the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plenty of ammunition to pursue more aggressive monetary policy. (AM)\par \par \uc2 GERMANY: October industrial orders rose 2.5% on the month against expectations of a 0.5% rise.\par \par \uc2 A surge in domestic orders boosted German industry in October. The data tend to be volatile, so it's too soon to say whether this might be the beginnings of a recovery, but the signs are hopeful. What's more, if orders are matched by rising domestic demand, this could signal the beginnings of a rebalancing within the eurozone. (AM)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 RUSSIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 : The \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ramped up \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the ruble, selling $2.6 billion in two days this week.\par \par \uc2 Having allowed the ruble to float, the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started to worry about its accelerating devaluation and so \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to put the brakes on. For now, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have stopped the ruble's declines. But as long as the trend in oil prices remains downward and Russia is in the grip of sanctions, the ruble's prospects will remain gloomy. (AM)\par \par \uc2 GERMANY: Bundesbank trimmed its forecasts for German growth to 1.4% for 2014 and 0.8% in 2015 from 1.4% and 1.8% in June.\par \par \uc2 The Bundesbank's acknowledgement that the German economy will be struggling for growth and that inflation will remain well below the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target next year ought to give the ECB ammunition to pursue vigorous monetary policy. Even so, it seems unlikely the Bundesbank will relax its ideological stance against further quantitative easing. (AM)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL: The IPCA consumer price index rose 0.51% in November from October and was up 6.56% on the year. The monthly increase represented an acceleration from the 0.42% rise posted in October, but was short of economists' for a 0.54% increase.\par \par \uc2 Inflation remains above the 6.5% ceiling of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil\uc2 's tolerance range for a fourth consecutive month. Translation: no end in sight for Brazil's cripplingly high interest rates. (MC)\par \par \uc2 COMING UP:\par \par \uc2 U.S.: 8:30 a.m. EST.\par \par \uc2 --October trade balance. [Expected $41.0 billion deficit vs. $43.0 billion in September.]\par \par \uc2 Growing U.S. exports of energy products is expected to have further reduced the trade deficit, providing even more compelling arguments for a stronger dollar. (MC)\par \par \uc2 --November labor report. [Expected payrolls +230,000 vs. +214,000 in October; unemployment rate expected 5.8%, unchanged from October.]\par \par \uc2 October's payroll report was a disappointment against so many other indicators of a tightening labor market, including a fall in the unemployment rate to its lowest level in six years. But some economists pointed out that there were some big seasonal adjustment effects in the data, which could mean the October payrolls number is in line for an upward revision. (MC)\par \par \uc2 To sign up for this email, click here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/MacroHorizonsSignup"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/MacroHorizonsSignup}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 05, 2014 07:19 ET (12:19 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141205eac5001k3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
