\par\par \b \uc2 Russian Ruble Falls to New Lows -- 2nd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrey Ostroukh   \par \uc2 361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2014\par 20:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK--The Russian ruble weakened to a record low Friday, as it continued to struggle under falling oil prices after \uc2 OPEC\uc2  members rejected calls for drastic action to cut their oil output.\par \par \uc2 The ruble touched a fresh low, with 50.03 rubles required to buy one dollar in afternoon trade during the New York session, before recovering slightly to 49.98. This takes the ruble's year-to-date depreciation against the dollar to 34%.\par \par \uc2 Responding to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp decline, the Bank of Russia said it would extend limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps in an effort to stabilize the ruble.\par \par \uc2 The decision by the \uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  to keep its production ceiling unchanged sent Brent crude prices below $70 per barrel in intraday trading for the first time since May 2010. The slump in oil prices threatens to further slow Russia's embattled economy as around 50% of the country's annual budget revenue stems from oil and gas exports.\par \par \uc2 Against the euro, the ruble eased to 62.24 before recovering slightly to 62.16.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Russia has allowed the ruble to float freely against the dollar and euro since early November, having previously \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem its loss in value.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had set a daily limit of $2 billion for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where banks receive rubles from the regulator using dollars and euros as collateral. This tool drew demand only recently after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  withdrew a large chunk of rubles from the interbank market as a result of $30 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October.\par \par \uc2 By extending the limit for the next two weeks on Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it hopes to deter speculators from betting on a weaker ruble.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had warned that it might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets in future to prop up the ruble, if it deemed it necessary for the sake of Russia's financial stability.\par \par \uc2 Write to Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 28, 2014 14:54 ET (19:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141128eabs002o1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
