\par\par \b \uc2 OPEC\uc2 : 'A Watershed for the Oil Market' -- Strategists React\b0\par\par\uc2 By Josie Cox   \par \uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2014\par 12:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 A watershed moment for oil markets? A disappointed for even the most pessimistic forecasts? The most unfriendly possible response to Russia's ruble?\par \par \uc2 Here's a selection of views from across the street a day after the \uc2 Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries\uc2  left its output target unchanged, diminishing hope of any imminent respite for the ailing commodity.\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  economists:\par \par \uc2 "The outcome of [the \uc2 OPEC\uc2 ] meeting marks a watershed for the oil market. In keeping the production target at 30 million barrels per day, \uc2 OPEC\uc2  is clearly signaling that it will no longer bear the burden of market adjustment alone and this decision puts the onus on other producers.\par \par \uc2 "Over the course of the coming months, oil markets will have to find a new equilibrium - a world where demand elasticities are tested, and non-OPEC supply sensitivities, and particularly the pain threshold for U.S. producers becomes better understood. At least another 1 million barrels per day of cuts looks necessary to balance the market, in our view."\par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Strategist Michael Hsueh:\par \par \uc2 "In our view today's decision throws the market balance into crisis most acutely in the first half of 2015 when seasonal demand is weak, and perhaps before U.S. tight oil producers have had a chance to make any major adjustment to activity levels. We estimate that sustained pricing at $60 [per barrel] of WTI is the level which would trigger a material shift in the trajectory of U.S. production growth."\par \par \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 Economist\uc2  Derek Halpenny:\par \par \uc2 "From an inflation perspective, this drop is more relevant for the ECB than other central banks seen as the ECB target rate includes energy prices. Energy makes up a little less than 10% of the euro-zone \uc2 CPI\uc2  basket and now Brent crude oil is down around 35% on an annual basis. If this drop is sustained through the remainder of the year, energy inflation is set to become a bigger drag on overall inflation. The annual rate is currently at -2.0% as of October and this could drop to -4.0/4.5% over the coming months."\par \par \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  strategists:\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 OPEC\uc2  yesterday delivered just about the most unfriendly possible response to the ruble and oil prices, essentially abdicating its role as chief global swing producer. Oil prices and the ruble plunged as the outcome disappointed even the most pessimistic of forecasts. The cartel has chosen to allow the market to find its own level to match supply with demand. Overshot or not, for Russia a weaker ruble is the main escape valve for lower oil prices, helping exporters and the government to balance its books.\par \par \uc2 "Whether or not [the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in huge amounts will be dictated by its judgment of whether 'financial stability' is at risk. It is hard to argue that it is not, but the hurdle to ad hoc \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has proven high to date."\par \par \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia Strategist Peter Dragicevich\par \par \uc2 "Falling crude oil prices coupled with lower long term U.S. interest rates mean that the \uc2 Fed\uc2  can tolerate a stronger U.S. dollar despite sluggish global economic activity. It is also important to note that declining oil prices will lower short--term inflation but are also a medium--term tailwind for consumption--led demand. A fall in oil prices means a fall in petrol prices, a fall in transport costs, and should be treated as a tax cut for consumers."\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 28-11-14 1157GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020141128eabs0009i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
