\par\par \b \uc2 Ruble Is Real Sanction For Russia -- Heard On The Street\b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2014\par 01:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C12\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 11/12/14) \par    By Richard Barley \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Russian ruble isn't out of the woods yet.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell nearly 8% against the dollar last week and is now down 29.4% year to date, a move that has started to raise concerns about financial stability. That is despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  raising interest rates by four percentage points this year to 9.5% and spending around $30 billion in October alone trying to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come up with a new strategy. It is allowing the ruble to float freely. That is an improvement on its previous regular, piecemeal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Those just provided a target for bets against the ruble. But even though \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of more than $400 billion, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  still needs to be credible and could backfire.\par \par \uc2 Beyond that, the most important factor to watch is a new program of one-year auctions of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This could supply up to $50 billion by the end of 2016. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold the first auction Nov. 17. If that goes well, it could calm ruble fears.\par \par \uc2 There are risks. There may not be sufficient collateral available for banks to use; logistics and bureaucracy also could intrude. A previous program's first auction flopped because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and banks failed to complete required paperwork, \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  notes.\par \par \uc2 Longer term, much will depend on how long Western sanctions against Russia last. For now, the greater demand for dollars appears to be coming from Russian banks and companies that have lost access to international financial markets but need to service foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt. To the end of August, the share of ruble-denominated deposits held by organizations excluding banks fell to 56.9% versus 62.3% a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 A bigger concern would be if individuals started to convert ruble holdings into dollars en masse. Data to the end of August show no sign of this. But if expectations of further ruble declines build, it could become a big issue.\par \par \uc2 As long as sanctions persist, demand for dollars will continue. And the economic outlook is only getting cloudier, as the oil-price declines and prospects for foreign investment diminish. Inflation is running above 8% while growth is stagnating.\par \par \uc2 Russia's confrontation with the West seems only to be intensifying. So the ruble will remain a cause for concern.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 11, 2014 19:49 ET (00:49 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141112eabc0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
