\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-China deal sends shares to 1-month high, ruble surges\b0\par\par\uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2014\par 22:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, new quotes)\par \par \uc2 * Long-awaited HK-Shanghai connection boosts Chinese shares\par \par \uc2 * Ruble up 2 pct after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  scraps formal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By David Gaffen\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 10 (Reuters) - World equity markets hit their highest level in more than a month on Monday, lifted by a deal to give global investors easier access to China's $3.9 trillion stock market and more gains in U.S. equities.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street ended higher, with the S&P 500 rising 0.3 percent to 2,038.26, a closing record, while the Dow industrials gained 0.2 percent to a new closing high of 17,613.74.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CBOE\uc2  Volatility Index, or VIX, Wall Street's favored index of anxiety, hit its lowest level since mid-September.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's All-World index, which spans 45 countries, rose 0.4 percent to hit its highest level since late September. European shares were up 0.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Interest rates are still low, oil prices are low and everything looks conducive to more strength between now and the end of the year," said \uc2 John Kosar\uc2 , director of research with Asbury Research in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Chinese shares jumped 2.5 percent and Hong Kong's \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  index climbed almost 1 percent overnight after officials announced a Nov. 17 start date for a long-awaited tie-up that will allow global investors to buy Chinese stocks from Hong Kong. Shares in those markets were the strongest among major world markets.\par \par \uc2 In Russia, tough talk from President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and a move by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to abandon rules-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent the ruble soaring after a recent run of weakness.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index added to recent gains, reversing early losses, and was up 0.2 percent as investors continue to favor the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the lower-yielding units of Japan and the euro zone. The dollar index, which measures the dollar against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rose to 87.82 and is up 12 percent since May. \par \par \uc2 There was no sign that recent volatility in Russia's ruble was about to let up. After dabbling with the idea last week, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  formally abolished structured \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After a dramatic fall in the previous week and volatile swings of 6 percent on Friday, the ruble was last up almost 1.7 percent at 45.89 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasuries sold off modestly, with the 10-year yield rising to 2.36 percent on a 17/32 point decline in the price of the benchmark.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices initially rose on renewed political tensions in the Middle East and Ukraine. But they subsequently gave up their gains, with Brent crude off 1.5 percent at $82.70, and U.S. light crude off 1.7 percent at $77.63. (Additional reporting by Hideyuki Sano in Tokyo; Editing by Hugh Lawson and Dan Grebler) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-11-10T22:23:53.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141110eaba00p5l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
