\par\par \b \uc2 Russian Ruble Recovers on  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Move -- 2nd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrey Ostroukh   \par \uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2014\par 17:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--Russia's battered ruble recovered Monday after President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  dismissed its recent drop as "speculative" and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would allow the rate to float freely in the market, reducing its regular \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and tightening supplies of rubles to discourage local investors from betting against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We're seeing some speculative jumps in the rate but I think this should come to an end in the nearest future in light of the actions the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is taking in response to speculators," Mr. Putin told a business conference early Monday in Beijing, where he is on a visit.\par \par \uc2 Shortly afterward, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced that it would eliminate the trading corridor it has been setting for the ruble and the regular \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it reached the boundaries. In recent weeks, that mechanism has done little to stem the ruble's slide, but cost the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly $30 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In its place, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when it sees "threats to financial stability."\par \par \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's chairwoman, told Rossiya-24 state television Monday that the bank's reserves are "adequate." She explained the decision to end regular \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a recognition of reality, saying that "if we spend [reserves] unwisely, trying to fight fundamental market trends, would lead us to the same result, only a bit later and with lower reserves."\par \par \uc2 "The drop in the ruble rate over the last few days and its excessive fluctuations have nothing to do with economic fundamentals," Ms. Nabiullina said. "In our view, the ruble rate was deeply undervalued compared to an equilibrium level."\par \par \uc2 In trading Monday, the ruble extended a recovery that began late Friday after hitting record lows against the dollar and euro at the end of last week. By the end of the trading session on the Moscow exchange the ruble traded at 45.53 a dollar and 56.61 a euro, compared with lows of more than 48 against the dollar and 60 against the euro on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is having an effect on the ruble: the threat of sizable one-off \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be working, " said \uc2 Tatiana Orlova\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 . "Also, President Putin threw his weight behind the [\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's] policy shift this morning, which I think could help to stabilize the population's expectations."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Nabiullina said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will limit its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to situations where needed to "break panic, speculative demand." In addition, the bank will limit lending of rubles to commercial banks, "which unfortunately have been used not only to finance the economy but also for 'games' on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market."\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Anton Siluanov\uc2  praised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to the free float but scolded the bank for not doing it earlier.\par \par \uc2 "I think that the decision came a bit too late as there was no need in having a trading corridor and spending reserves when the pressure on the ruble emerged," Mr. Siluanov said.\par \par \uc2 The extreme market moves and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's shifting responses--as recently as last week, the bank left the corridor mechanism in place but reduced its regular \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounts--underline how the combination of western sanctions and the falling price of oil, Russia's main export, have whipsawed the country's economy and financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Also Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  downgraded its forecast for economic growth and upped its expectation for capital outflow this year and warned that it now expects sanctions to remain in place through 2017. Even so, the bank said it expects Russia to be able to avoid a deep recession unless oil prices fall significantly further.\par \par \uc2 "The Russian economy will be stable even if oil prices remain at the level of $80 a barrel in 2015-17 and mutual sanctions are not canceled," first deputy chairwoman Kseniya Yudaeva said in a statement. But she said the bank's baseline scenario calls for stagnation over the next two years after growth of 0.3% this year--the weakest performance since the 1990s, and down from its previous estimates of 0.4%. A drop in oil prices to $60 a barrel--something few forecasters expect--would trigger a deeper recession and further declines in the ruble, the bank warned.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said it expects capital outflows to reach $128 billion this year, the highest since 2008, and fall back only slightly next year to $99 billion.\par \par \uc2 That outlook is a big reason why economists expect pressure on the ruble to remain despite the latest moves.\par \par \uc2 "The CBR's limitations on ruble liquidity may help to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the short term. However, ruble volatility and weakness may persist until there is more clarity on the CBR's appetite for deploying discretionary \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while longer-term ruble depreciation may continue on deteriorating fundamentals," said Phoenix Kalen, strategist at \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Alexander Kolyandr in Moscow and Chiara Albanese in London contributed to this item.\par \par \uc2 Write to Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 10, 2014 11:37 ET (16:37 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141110eaba002u7\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Russia CB Allows Ruble Free Float; Cuts Growth Forecast\b0\par\par\uc2 513 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2014\par \uc2 RTT News\par \uc2 RTTNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2014 NoticiasFinancieras. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (RTTNews) - \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday allowed the ruble to float freely, after it fell heavily in recent weeks, and cut the country's economic growth forecasts on the premise that the Western sanctions would remain until the end of 2017.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Russia announced in a statement that it was abandoning the ruble's trading corridor and regular foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "However, the new approach of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the domestic market does not provide for complete abandonment of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which can be implemented in case of financial stability threats," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "As a result of the implementation of this decision, the ruble exchange rate will be determined by the market factors, that should enhance the efficiency of the Bank of Russia monetary policy and ensure price stability."\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the bank had decided to reduce its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to $350 million per day. The ruble depreciated to record lows against the US dollar and the euro last week. On Monday, the ruble recovered some lost ground following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcement.\par \par \uc2 Late October, the bank raised its key rate by a massive 150 basis points to 9.50 percent to counteract the slide in ruble and rising inflation. The rate has been hiked by a cumulative 400 basis points since February.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials including Governor \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2  today described the slide in the ruble as speculative and asserted that it has nothing to do with economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 In an interview to Rossiya 24 channel, Nabiullina said the bank will temporarily limit the ruble liquidity supply, to reduce further speculative action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also cut the growth forecast for the Russian economy, predicting zero growth for the next year and just a 0.1 percent expansion for 2016. Previously, the bank projected 1 percent growth for next year.\par \par \uc2 The downgrade comes amid the recent slump in oil prices that add to woes already caused by the Western sanctions on Russia after the standoff with Ukraine. The bank expects the sanctions to remain in place until the end of 2017.\par \par \uc2 Russian economy will be stable even if the oil price will remain at the level of $80 per barrel in 2015-2017 and mutual sanctions will not be cancelled, the official news agency TASS cited \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  First Deputy Chairman Ksenia Yudayeva, as saying on Monday during the publication of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy guidelines for the next three years.\par \par \uc2 The inflation forecast for the next year was cut to 6.2-6.4 percent from 4.5-5 percent. The bank raised its forecast for capital outflow for this year to $128 billion from $90 billion. Further, the forecast on the average price of Urals crude blend in 2015 was slashed to $95 from $104.8 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 For comments and feedback: contact editorial@rttnews.com\par \par \uc2 Copyright(c) 2014 RTTNews.com. All Rights Reserved\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTTNEW0020141110eaba004jx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
