\par\par \b \uc2 Ruble 'Not Out Of The Woods'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chiara Albanese and Josie Cox   \par \uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2014\par 14:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Is the Russian ruble getting a reprieve?\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has clearly not been having a good year. A fall of 8% against the U.S. dollar last week brought its decline for the year to date against the dollar to about 30% as the impact of sanctions and a fall in the price of oil have combined to weigh heavily on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But its freefall came to a halt Monday, with the ruble bouncing up almost 3% after \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated that it stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time.\par \par \uc2 How? Well, in brief the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float freely instead of maintaining strict limits before it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it sees "threats to financial stability" and would limit lending of rubles to commercial banks, because it thinks some of that money has been used to play "games" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Is the worst over? Here is what analysts say:\par \par \uc2 Piotr Matys, emerging market strategist at \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 : "We are seeing some relief in the short-term but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is certainly not out of the woods. What we've seen so far is plenty of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but in my view it is inevitable that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  properly and sell as well. It is of paramount importance that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is stabilized to limit financial instability. My base case is also that we will have to see another aggressive interest rate hike, perhaps of as much as 200 basis points. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own might not be enough."\par \par \uc2 Oleg Kouzmin, strategist at Renaissance Capital: "The ruble is feeling better, supported by the Putin's comments and accommodating to a modified free floating framework. We hope ruble would be around 41-42 per dollar next year even with oil between $90-80 a barrel. But if we are wrong and goes anywhere possible, we do not think that any elements of capital controls would be introduced."\par \par \uc2 Phoenix Kalen, strategist at \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\uc2 : "The CBR's limitations on ruble liquidity may help to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the short term. However, ruble volatility and weakness may persist until there is more clarity on the CBR's appetite for deploying discretionary \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while longer term ruble depreciation may continue on deteriorating fundamentals."\par \par \uc2 Piotr Chwiejczak, strategist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 : "What the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has done in terms of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is certainly a step in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 "It is also significant that President Putin himself has talked specifically about the dollar for the first time. This shows that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation it has become a national security issue and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is indeed prepared to do a lot more if necessary. The situation is still very unstable in Russia and Ukraine and the ruble trend has certainly not been reversed for good, but the worst appears to be over."\par \par \uc2 Evgeny Gavrilenkov, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 : "The measures are what was needed in order to save the ruble and move away from a free fall regime to a free float. Most important was that the CBR announced intentions not to provide additional liquidity to banks. If the CBR will be consistent, then the ruble will continue appreciating and will eventually stabilize on a more equilibrium level, which will reduce the support to the idea of capital controls." \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 10, 2014 08:17 ET (13:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141110eaba0026f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
