\par\par \b \uc2 Russia's Ulyukayev Says Ruble Oversold, Set to Recover -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrey Ostroukh   \par \uc2 484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2014\par 13:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--The Russian ruble is oversold and is set to recover in the near future, Economy Minister Alexei Ulyukayev said Saturday in another verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at supporting the battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Russian news agencies reported.\par \par \uc2 The ruble has been hitting fresh record lows for more than a month, shedding around 10% in just three trading days this month following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to scale down \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Finance Ministry have also said recently the ruble has weakened beyond its fair price and is expected to regain some strength.\par \par \uc2 "I think that the ruble is really oversold. One may expect a move in the opposite direction in the pretty near future," Mr. Ulyukayev said to reporters during a trip to Beijing.\par \par \uc2 The ruble recovered slightly from record lows by the end of trading in Moscow on Friday, with traders saying the rebound might have been caused by profit-taking and on expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to stabilize the market.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement emailed to The Wall Street Journal on Friday that the ruble was undervalued and that the bank had "enough instruments to influence the exchange rate" and it was "ready to act at any moment."\par \par \uc2 Battered by Western sanctions and falling prices for oil, the ruble has lost around 30% against the dollar this year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent some $30 billion to slow the ruble's depreciation in October but earlier this week said it would scale down \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , effectively letting the ruble float almost freely.\par \par \uc2 The ruble's depreciation may add up to 4 percentage points to the headline reading of stubbornly high consumer inflation, according to Mr. Ulyukayev.\par \par \uc2 He also said the ruble's drop won't spur economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Russia is living through a so-called stagflation--a mix of sluggish economic growth and rising prices. This year, the economy is on track to grow by some 0.5%, down from 1.3% last year, which would be its lowest reading in five years. The pace of growth contrasts with nearly a decade of average annual growth of 7% and ambitious calls from President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to keep the economy growing by some 5% a year.\par \par \uc2 Inflation this year has already overshot the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ceiling of 6.5%, spurred by the weaker ruble and Moscow's ban of food imports from countries that sanctioned Russia.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ulyukayev, who served as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first deputy chairman for nearly a decade until last year, said Saturday he supports the bank's commitment to let the ruble to float freely and let the market steer the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Write to Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 08, 2014 07:35 ET (12:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141108eab8000f3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
