\par\par \b \uc2 Ruble: Watch Out Below\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chiara Albanese and Tommy Stubbington   \par \uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2014\par 13:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Poor old ruble. The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lurched lower yet again Friday, in what local traders describe as increasingly nervous market conditions.\par \par \uc2 This is upsetting other markets too. The ruble bond index from \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  is down about 8% since the start of November, and down 17% since the start of October.\par \par \uc2 Russian corporate bonds are feeling the heat too. \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  bonds maturing March 2022, for instance, are now yielding 6.3%, almost 0.2 percentage point more than Thursday's close, according to Tradeweb. (Yields rise when prices fall.) Banks are also under pressure. \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 's bonds maturing May 2023 are yielding 7.6%, roughly 0.2 percentage point higher than Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Will the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pounce and try to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under its new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management regime? The market suspects so.\par \par \uc2 But the longer term picture for the ruble is grim.\par \par \uc2 Here's what ruble watchers expect next:\par \par \uc2 Evgeny Gavrilenkov, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 : "Until now, most of the dollar demand was coming from banks and corporates which have to repay the debt and speculative bets expecting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When local households add to the pressure, this becomes a problem, and this is what we are seeing here. The overall demand is exceeding the $350 million limit the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is willing to sell. If the ruble keeps weakening, the demand is about that and mostly coming from households."\par \par \uc2 Ivan Tchakarov, chief economist for Russia at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 : "Until October 1, which is the latest data the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  provides, the demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't coming from households but mainly from corporates and banks that were facing big upcoming obligations. I wouldn't be surprised if the retail demand picked up in October given the rout we've been seeing in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. What we've been seeing in the last couple of days isn't related to any fundamental change in the economy. Rather there have been headlines about more fighting in Donetsk and talk of further sanctions. It's unfortunate for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has happened exactly at the time they changed their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy."\par \par \uc2 Alina Slyusarchuk, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 : "Weakness is now driven by speculative demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is true that with sanctions there should be high demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  to repay maturing external debt, however, we are skeptical that this is driving ruble at the moment as the CBR has provided almost $30 billion through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On balance, we have seen ruble weakness as driven by concerns over the credibility of monetary policy, sanctions and a lower oil price. While concerns over monetary policy have been addressed, we see higher uncertainty on sanctions and oil still remains an unknown."\par \par \uc2 Phoenix Kalen, emerging market strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 : "We don't have a breakdown between corporates, households and financial institutions, but we believe that currently, the main demand for the dollar comes from the Russian population. We estimate that these panic purchases have amounted to $20 billion to 30 billion. While the oil price has serious implications for commodity exporting countries, Russia stands out as a special case due to the current sensitive geopolitical situation."\par \par \uc2 Oleg Kouzmin, Russia economist at Renaissance Capital: "The CBR changed the rules of the game, and market tries to adjust itself to the new life without the floating band. It looks like the general flow from the whole market, but I hope that households do not take a big part now."\par \par \uc2 -- Ben Edwards contributed to this post. \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 07, 2014 07:14 ET (12:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141107eab70024g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
