\par\par \b \uc2 Ruble Dive "Disturbing" -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2014\par 09:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0843 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Another day, another collapse in the ruble. USD is now at RUB48; EUR at RUB60. It's "quite incredible," says Tim Ash at \uc2 Standard Bank\uc2  "and where is the CBR? This is a huge, huge move. The dollar is strong generally at the moment, and maybe the CBR has decided that trying to defend the ruble against a broader dollar rally is worthless, but the scale of the move is now disturbing." (katie.martin@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 0830 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Erste Bank cuts its forecast for Hungary's industrial output growth this year even deeper in light of September's lower-than-expected data. Erste now sees this year's output growth at 8.5%, having lowered it already in early October to 8.9% from 10.8%. In September, output increased 7.6% annually and the working-day adjusted increase was 5.2% on the year, both lower than Erste's forecasts for 9.1% and 6.5%, respectively. "Although production increased 2.7% on a monthly basis, it is still weak and underpins our suspicion that there was not only a one-off effect behind the 5.7% drop in August," Erste adds. (margit.feher@wsj.com @margitfeher) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0825 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The ruble has lost more than 10% in just a week, and around a third so far this year, against the dollar, sliding to its weakest ever level of 48.22 per dollar on Friday. Early weakening Friday was followed by a quick rebound to 47.50 per dollar, which has sparked rumors that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could have been behind the move. But that's unlikely as the volumes were thin, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 ING\f28 \uc2 \f2 . It says the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needs to carry out massive and unexpected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over time to reverse the ruble's trend. "The more the regulator waits, the harder it will be to find a solution capable of stabilizing the market... It is difficult to stop panic among households," warns \f28 \uc2 \f2 ING\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0818 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/TWD jumps to a three-year high onshore close Friday, as the Taiwan dollar continues to follow the yen's depreciation. The local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is suspected of buying greenbacks Friday afternoon to lift the pair from around 30.620 to an onshore close of 30.716, versus Thursday's close of 30.605, says a local trader. "The Taiwan dollar has been depreciating at a slower rate than regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the yen, so the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been working to weaken the Taiwan dollar," the trader says. The pair is likely to continue rising next week, and may also be affected by upcoming U.S. non-farm payroll data, the trader says. (eva.dou@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0812 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China needs to cut its interest rates to ease businesses' high borrowing costs, but the chance of an imminent rate cut is slim, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists say. The latest policy report released by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Thursday suggests that authorities prefer to use other policy tools such as liquidity \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to reduce borrowing costs, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  says in a note to clients. The economists still believe that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  needs to cut benchmark interest rates three times by the end of 2015. (liyan.qi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0808 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Firms from China and Japan all want to participate in the high-speed rail project linking Kuala Lumpur and Singapore, says Hamid Albar, chairman of Malaysia's Land Public Transport Commission. "The Chinese will certainly say that we can do it cheaper, although obviously their track record is shorter. But they will say they built more in the shorter period of time," said Song Seng Wun an analyst with CIMB. China introduced high-speed rail in 2007. Japan is banking on its experience and technical know-how in building the Shinkansen in the 1960s. Other countries keen to participate are Spain, France, Germany and South Korea. The High Speed Rail, estimated to cost 40 billion ringgit, will cut the travel time between Kuala Lumpur and Singapore to 90 minutes. Its construction is expected to start late next year. (celine.fernandez@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0806 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were pushed weaker against the U.S. dollar Friday, with some hitting multi-month lows as the ICE DXY U.S. Dollar Index hit a four year high as policy surprises from central banks dominated market movements. The South Korean won hit its lowest in over a year as the Malaysian ringgit fell to its weakest in eight months against the dollar and the Philippine Peso touched a seven month low. This, analysts say, is a fallout of the weaker Japanese yen, after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's surprise policy announcement last week. The weak yen poses a threat to the competitiveness of regional economies. Dovish comments from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday sent the Euro to a two-year low. "While \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  easing has traditionally been viewed as risk-positive, in the current environment the opposite could be the case," analysts from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  wrote in a note. Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "are currently seeing their competitive positions deteriorate, thanks to EUR and JPY weakness," they noted. The DXY index touched 88.174 Friday and last traded at 88.016.(anjani.trivedi@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @anjani_trivedi) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0759 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Overnight EUR/USD just managed to hold above its post-Draghi low seen Thursday, while the USD made fresh multi-year highs vs AUD, NZD. Stocks enjoyed the ECB President's dovish words with the S&P, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Nikkei +0.5% and change. In Asia trading ranges for the major pairs were tight as is usual pre-payrolls, where the DowJones survey forecast is +233K, although an above-forecast \f28 \uc2 \f2 ADP\f28 \uc2 \f2  print and the 9-year high ISM non-manufacturing employment reading suggest upside risks here. Beforehand, the data slate offers UK Sept trade balance (0930 GMT), already out German Sept industrial output disappointed at +1.4% m-m against 2% expected, while French industrial production was flat vs -0.2% forecast. gary.stride@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0756 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--It's refreshing when an economist admits he (or she) isn't totally sure of what's happening. \f28 \uc2 \f2 ING\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Carsten Brzeski said Friday that recently, "the German economy has been a mystery." On the one hand, there's "partly terrifying data", especially from industry, yet there's also "anecdotal evidence and undebatable solid economic fundamentals," that seem to tell another story. Still, "it would be naive to simply deny clearly disappointing data." But he said it's hard to know what's really going on in Europe's largest economy. The question is, is Germany's slowdown a result of volatility from one-offs like weather, vacations and school holidays or is it the result of weaknesses in trading partners and the end of a growth cycle? "In our view, it is a combination of the two," he said. Perhaps fittingly, he says calling 3Q GDP "is not an easy bet." todd.buell@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0751 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--ECB President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2 's comments Thursday of returning the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's balance sheet to the near EUR3 trillion level seen in March 2012 put large scale buying of European Government bonds back on the cards for early 2015 says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The bank says that the current purchase programs won't be enough to reach the stated goal while the other contingency for expanding QE - a further worsening of the inflation outlook - will also likely be triggered. (nick.cawley@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0732 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]---The ruble's rapid drop to fresh record lows, a process repeated almost daily for more than a month, brought panic onto the market, says VTB 24, a retail arm of Russia's No.2 lender VTB. "A further ruble weakening starts to threaten stability of the financial system," VTB 24 says. It expects that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will have to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the market soon to stabilize the ruble. Until then VTB 24 recommends scaling down exposure to shares in Russian non-export companies with big loans in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0724 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--German data nerds woke up this morning to a bit of a mixed bag. Industrial production on the whole was weaker than expected in September, printing a +1.4% on the month, disappointing the Wall Street Journal economist forecast of a 2.0%. However, after Thursday's small gain in industrial orders, a miss in production wasn't all that surprising. Plus, the key capital goods category rose by 4.5% in September, though that followed a decline of 7.9%, potentially signaling strength in the important heavy machinery sector. And, perhaps to be expected, Germany's mighty export sector may be back on track. It grew by 5.5% on the month in September, almost making up for the 5.8% drop in August that, along with other bad data for that month, scared investors. (todd.buell@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 07, 2014 03:43 ET (08:43 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141107eab7001f9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
